  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "event_data_buffer_lifetime",
      "result": "normal_remove_path_is_synchronized; no new candidate-specific race established",
      "evidence": [
        "The warning load at syna_dev_remove offset 0x2e8 addresses event_data lock-depth: buffer base offset 0x2a8 plus byte offset 0x40.",
        "syna_dev_isr passes this event_data buffer to syna_tcm_get_event_data, which copies through syna_tcm_buf_copy.",
        "Fresh extraction from the current module shows syna_tcm_buf_copy matches the stock 640-byte function, instructions and relocations; stock locks both buffers and unlocks them on return paths.",
        "Remove calls syna_dev_disconnect before freeing event_data. The stock disconnect body is exact in the current module and releases the requested IRQ before clearing connected state; IRQ release synchronizes the handler before the later buffer free.",
        "Stock syna_dev_remove logs a nonzero lock depth, resets the byte, and continues teardown. The candidate preserves this. A nonzero count is a diagnostic abnormality; static evidence did not establish a reachable normal-path race."
      ],
      "remaining_uncertainty": "No in-kernel race detector or NX809J hardware run was performed; abnormal nonzero lock-depth teardown still follows stock free-after-warning behavior."
    },
    {
      "id": "duplicate_disconnect_and_charger_stop",
      "result": "sequential duplicate in platform remove is idempotent under the observed connected-state guard and lifecycle state",
      "evidence": [
        "Candidate syna_cdev_remove calls syna_dev_disconnect, then syna_dev_remove calls it again after syna_cdev_remove returns.",
        "The stock disconnect checks the connected byte and clears it after cleanup. Its 392-byte body was freshly extracted from the current module and matches stock instructions and relocations; the second sequential call takes the disconnected path.",
        "The wrapper syna_dev_disconnect calls syna_tpd_charger_lifecycle_stop before the stock body. stop serializes state under a mutex, marks stopping, clears registration state, cancels startup work synchronously, unregisters the notifier, cancels/destroys the charger queue, and clears/puts the cached power_supply. Repeating it for the same tcm observes the resources already absent.",
        "The remove path stops helper-queue admission under a spinlock, then cancel_work_sync and flush/destroy drain helper work before object teardown."
      ],
      "remaining_uncertainty": "Conclusion is for the synchronous remove call chain; no concurrent runtime stress was performed."
    },
    {
      "id": "cdev_and_input_agent_operations",
      "result": "post-quiesce callbacks are gated and retained mmap storage is reference-counted",
      "evidence": [
        "syna_cdev_quiesce clears online and advances generation under the lifecycle mutex, wakes waiters, and waits until active-operation count reaches zero.",
        "The current module contains the extracted 336-byte syna_cdev_quiesce implementation; native read/write/ioctl/open/release and misc poll/ioctl/mmap paths use operation admission before touching tcm context.",
        "The input-agent owner detaches its tcm pointer on retirement and retains its buffer through file/VMA references; release calls syna_release, whose body only logs, then drops the owner reference."
      ],
      "remaining_uncertainty": "Static review does not validate all userspace ioctl/mmap interleavings on hardware."
    }
